  overview: "Pulled elbow, also called nursemaid's elbow, is a radial head subluxation in young children. It usually follows a sudden pull on the extended, pronated forearm, which draws the radius through the annular ligament [r1]. It can also follow a fall or a twist [r2]. The child feels sudden pain and loses function in the arm, and holds it still without swelling or deformity. It is the most common cause of upper limb immobility in infants and preschool children presenting to paediatric emergency departments [r3]. In a series of 2331 cases the frequency peaked at 6 months and 2 years, boys and girls were equally affected, the left arm was more often involved and about half the cases followed forcible traction of the forearm [r4].\n\nThe diagnosis is clinical: a child of toddler age who guards the arm after a pull, without swelling, bruising or bony tenderness. Half of children, however, present after an unwitnessed injury or with an atypical history, and then a fracture may be the cause of the pain [r5]. In an ultrasound series of 50 children, 78% had an intact but interposed annular ligament and 22% had a torn ligament [r1]. Radiographs are usually unnecessary, but 5 of 90 children with a clinical diagnosis in one reduction trial turned out to have a fracture [r6, r7].\n\nReduction is a quick manoeuvre, and the question is which one. A Cochrane review of 9 trials (906 children) found low-quality evidence that hyperpronation failed less often at the first attempt than supination-flexion (9.2% versus 26.4%); evidence on pain was very low quality, and adverse effects and recurrence were not reported [r2]. A meta-analysis of 7 randomised trials reached the same conclusion [r8]. Recurrence is common enough to matter: 14% in the large series and 6% in an ultrasound series [r4, r1].",

