    text: "The radial head sits against the capitellum at the radiocapitellar joint and is held against the ulna by the annular ligament, a ring of fibres that encircles the radial head and neck (standard anatomy). In a young child the ossification of the radial head is incomplete and the annular ligament is thin (standard anatomy; the primary anatomical studies were not retrievable for this page). In a pulled elbow the radius slides distally through the ligament [r2]. On ultrasound the ligament may be absent from its normal place, interposed, or torn [r1], and in one study the supinator shape change, the position of the annular ligament and the synovial fringe were examined [r3].",
