    tissueDamage: "Sudden longitudinal traction on the extended, pronated forearm draws the radius distally while the annular ligament, which is anchored to the ulna, stays behind. The ligament then slips over the radial head [r2, r1]. In 50 children examined with static and dynamic ultrasound, 39 (78%) had an intact but interposed annular ligament, and 11 (22%) had a torn ligament [r1]. A separate ultrasound study defined the diagnosis by the absence of the annular ligament from its normal place, with sensitivity 64.9% and specificity 100% [r3].\n\nMost children respond dramatically to reduction, while some improve more slowly, and the ultrasound classification offers an explanation: children with a torn ligament were splinted for 7 days in that series [r1]. The injury is a mechanical displacement rather than a bony or major soft-tissue injury, so there is no cellular healing cascade for most children (clinical reasoning); a torn ligament would heal through the standard ligament cascade (typical biology).",
